 ],
 "literal": "Small-flowered ñāḻal tree's root-hollow resting place / of the crab, its small house, so as to destroy, the wave / like a drum struck by a drumstick, moves, the lord of the shore; / the days he granted us are very few, but the public gossip / is like the bow-bearing army's chief of the Vicciyar, / when he fought with the kings, the bards / who saw the tiger-like fierce stance, / raised a clamor in noisy Kurumpur, and this gossip is even greater than that!",
 "translation": "The waves crash like drums struck by drumsticks, destroying the tiny homes built by crabs in the hollows of the roots of the small-flowered ñāḻal trees on the shore of our lord. The days he spent lovingly with us are very few indeed. Yet, the gossip about us is louder than the great uproar that rose in the noisy town of Kurumpur, when the bards witnessed the fierce, tiger-like battle stance of the great chief of the bow-wielding Vicciyar army fighting against the great kings.",
 "modern": "சிறிய பூக்களைக் கொண்ட ஞாழல் மரத்தின் வேர் வளைகளில் நண்டுகள் கட்டிய சிறிய வீடுகள் அழியும்படி, முரசு போல ஒலிக்கும் அலைகள் மோதும் கடற்கரைத் தலைவன் நம்மோடு கூடி மகிழ்ந்த நாட்கள் மிகச் சிலவே. ஆனால், வில்லேந்திய படையையுடைய விச்சியர் தலைவன் மூவேந்தர்களுடன் போரிட்டபோது, அவனது புலி போன்ற வீரத் தோற்றத்தைக் கண்ட பாணர்கள் ஆரவாரித்த ஒலியை விட, ஆரவாரம் மிக்க குறும்பூர் நகரில் எழுந்த அலர் (வதந்தி) மிகப் பெரியதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "In this neytal (maritime) poem, the confidante (tōḻi) addresses the heroine, or speaks in the hearing of the secret lover, about the rising public gossip (alar). She points out that the actual days of union granted by the lord of the shore (tuṟaivaṉ) were very few, yet the rumors have grown disproportionately loud. The poem uses a vivid natural image: the waves, sounding like drums beaten with drumsticks, crash and destroy the tiny homes built by crabs in the root-hollows of the ñāḻal trees. This represents how the harsh public gossip threatens to destroy the delicate, secret love of the couple. To emphasize the scale of the gossip, the poet Paraṇar employs a historical allusion, comparing the uproar to the clamor raised by bards in the town of Kurumpur upon witnessing the fierce, tiger-like battle stance of the Vicciyar chief fighting against the kings.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
